<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willibaldsburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Willibaldsburg is a hilltop castle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, the residence of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Eichstätt from the 14th to the 18th century. Rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style around 1600 by Elias Holl, it held the famous garden recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hortus-eystettensis">Hortus Eystettensis</a>, recreated in 1998, and now houses museums.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willibaldsburg.jpg" alt="Willibaldsburg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joe MiGo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willibaldsburg_%C3%BCber_Eichst%C3%A4tt.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle stands west of the old town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> on the Willibaldsberg, a ridge 464 m above sea level inside a sharp bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a>, and takes its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibald">Willibald</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Eichstätt. Fortifications on the hill are mentioned as early as 1070. After the Counts of Hirschberg died out in 1305 the bishops had to see to the defence of their diocese themselves, and around 1355 Bishop Berthold von Zollern began turning the site into a fortified residence, against the objections of the cathedral chapter to its cost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Within about fifty years the castle changed from a late medieval fortress into a stately residence. Bishop Martin von Schaumberg built a three-winged range on its eastern side, and Bishop Johann Konrad von Gemmingen laid out the botanical garden and from 1609 began a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace on the Italian model to plans by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> master builder Elias Holl. His successor Johann Christoph von Westerstetten completed it, adding the two-tower facade from 1629, five bastions and a large outer bailey.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> a Swedish army under Bernhard von Sachsen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> took the fortress in the spring of 1633, and a Bavarian army under Colonel Johann von Werth retook it that October. In 1725 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> moved to their new palace by the cathedral, and the castle housed offices and later a hospital and a prison. After the bishopric was secularised in 1806 it was sold and partly demolished; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> bought it back in 1829 and used it as barracks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> made the castle a cultural monument in 1900. After housing refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> from 1945 to 1955, it was restored from 1962 by the Bavarian Administration of State-Owned Palaces, Gardens and Lakes. The Jura Museum, opened in 1976, shows fossils including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> specimen of Archaeopteryx, and the Museum of Prehistory and Early History followed in 1980. The reconstructed bastion garden was opened in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willibaldsburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prince-bishop, elected by the cathedral chapter, governed from Eichstätt and the hilltop <b>Willibaldsburg</b>, where Johann Conrad von Gemmingen laid out the garden recorded in the Hortus Eystettensis of 1613.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-eichstatt">Prince-Bishopric of Eichstätt</a></li></ul></section>
